<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">9 May in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 9 May, from 1386 to 1960.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1386</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">England and Portugal seal their alliance at Windsor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Treaty of Windsor pledged perpetual friendship between the two kingdoms. The Anglo-Portuguese alliance is the oldest diplomatic alliance in the world still in force.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1671</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Thomas Blood tries to steal the Crown Jewels</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Disguised as a parson, Thomas Blood and his accomplices overpowered the keeper of the Jewel House and fled with the crown and orb, but were caught. Charles II surprisingly pardoned him.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1864</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Sniper kills Union general John Sedgwick at Spotsylvania</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Moments after telling his men that Confederate sharpshooters 'couldn't hit an elephant at this distance', General Sedgwick was shot dead. He was the highest-ranking Union officer killed in the war.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1926</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Byrd and Bennett claim to fly over the North Pole</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Richard Byrd and Floyd Bennett flew a Fokker trimotor from Spitsbergen and reported reaching the Pole. Doubts about their speed and navigation have kept the claim in dispute.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1950</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Robert Schuman proposes pooling Europe's coal and steel</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">France's foreign minister proposed placing French and German coal and steel under a common authority, making war between them 'materially impossible'. It led to the European Coal and Steel Community.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">Steel</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1960</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">FDA says it will approve the first contraceptive pill</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The US Food and Drug Administration announced it would license Enovid for contraception. The pill gave women far greater control over fertility and transformed family life.</p></li></ol>
